    "https://vcujobs.com/jobs/diagnostic-medical-physicist-som-m89935-mcv-main-campus-virginia-united-states": {
      "title": "Diagnostic Medical Physicist-SOM-M89935",
      "description": "Diagnostic Medical Physicist-SOM-M89935 - MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States Diagnostic Medical Physicist-SOM-M89935 - MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States Search Jobs Applicant Login Settings Kiosk mode Ready to join a workforce UN LIKE any other? Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Diagnostic Medical Physicist-SOM-M89935 45002563 907 Floyd Avenue, MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States, 23284 Faculty Clinical Faculty MCVP School Of Medicine MBU Advertising Summary: Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) School of Medicine is a premier academic medical center located in the heart of Richmond. Accounting for almost half of VCU's sponsored research, the School of Medicine is internationally recognized for patient care and education. Virginia Commonwealth University is an equal opportunity employer. Unit: School Of Medicine MBU Department: Radiology Department Summary: The Division of Diagnostic Medical Physics in the Department of Radiology at VCU Health is seeking a clinically oriented Diagnostic Medical Physicist to join our team of medical physicists. The candidate will provide comprehensive imaging physics support across VCU Health's hospitals and outpatient sites and contribute to our clinical, educational, and quality/safety missions. The position includes responsibilities in diagnostic imaging physics as well as support of nuclear medicine and molecular imaging services, including Y-90 radioembolization and other potential theranostic programs. Duties & Responsibilities: Clinical / Quality / Regulatory Provide medical physics support for all major imaging modalities, including: General radiography, fluoroscopy, interventional radiology, and mobile C-arms, CT, Mammography and digital breast tomosynthesis (DBT), MRI, and ultrasound Serve as the primary medical physics support for the Y-90 radioembolization program, including treatment planning support, dose verification activities, regulatory compliance, multidisciplinary coordination, and procedural coverage in collaboration with Interventional Radiology, Nuclear Medicine, and Radiation Safety personnel. Perform acceptance testing, commissioning, and periodic performance evaluations of diagnostic imaging equipment in accordance with state and federal regulations, ACR/MQSA/TJC requirements, and institutional policies. Develop, implement, and maintain quality assurance programs for imaging systems, including oversight of technologist-performed QC and use of enterprise QC management tools. Support and advise clinical staff on radiation safety, imaging protocols, CT dose optimization and tracking, fluoroscopic dose management, and MRI safety. Perform shielding design and evaluation for new or renovated imaging suites and verify compliance with applicable national standards and Virginia regulations. Prepare and maintain documentation to demonstrate compliance with: The Joint Commission (TJC) ACR accreditation programs (CT, mammography, MRI, ultrasound, nuclear medicine/PET, as applicable) FDA, MQSA, and state (VDH) regulatory requirements. Education / Training Participate in the didactic and clinical education of: Diagnostic radiology residents Graduate students in the CAMPEP-accredited Medical Physics program Medical physics residents (as applicable) Technologists and other clinical staff (e.g., modality-specific QC, dose optimization, and radiation safety in imaging). Contribute to curriculum development and formal course teaching in diagnostic medical physics as appropriate. Participate in the education and training of physicians, residents, technologists, nurses, and other clinical staff involved in nuclear medicine, molecular imaging, and theranostic procedures. Research / Scholarly Activity Collaborate with radiology, medical physics, and other departments on clinically focused imaging and radiation safety research projects, quality improvement initiatives, and protocol optimization studies. Mentor residents, fellows, and graduate students in scholarly projects when feasible. While this is a primarily clinical position, opportunities exist to participate in funded or unfunded research consistent with departmental priorities and candidate interest. Service Participate in departmental, School of Medicine, and health-system committees related to imaging quality, radiation safety, and accreditation. Contribute to professional organizations and continuing education activities as appropriate. Qualifications: Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree (M.S.) in Medical Physics, Radiological Physics, or a closely related discipline. Board certification in Diagnostic Radiological Physics / Diagnostic Medical Physics by the American Board of Radiology (ABR) or equivalent upon hire, or board eligible with the expectation of achieving certification within 2 years of hire. Demonstrated clinical experience in diagnostic medical / imaging physics in a hospital or health-system environment, including routine QA, acceptance testing, and regulatory/accreditation support. Demonstrated ability to work in and foster an environment of respect, professionalism, and civility with a population of faculty, staff, and students from all backgrounds and experiences, or a commitment to do so as a faculty member at VCU. Eligibility for permanent employment in the United States. Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. or D.Sc. in Medical Physics or related field. ABR certification in Nuclear Medical Physics or equivalent (strong plus, but not required). MQSA-qualified medical physicist with experience in mammography and DBT clinical programs. Experience with ACR accreditation processes for CT, mammography, MRI, ultrasound, and nuclear medicine, and familiarity with state and federal regulatory requirements for diagnostic imaging equipment. Experience with enterprise radiation dose monitoring, CT protocol management, and radiation safety program support. Operational knowledge and experience supporting nuclear medicine and hybrid imaging systems, including PET/CT and SPECT/CT, with the ability to provide coverage flexibility and collaborate in performance evaluations and surveys of these systems. Experience with radioactive materials programs, radiation safety principles, or theranostic procedures sufficient to support clinical nuclear medicine operations. Programming and data-analysis skills (i.e., MATLAB, Python, or similar) for protocol optimization, dose tracking, and image quality assessment. Prior academic teaching experience with radiology residents, medical physics trainees, or technologist programs. Salary Range: Commensurate FLSA Exemption Status: Exempt ORP Eligible: No Rank: Open Tenure: Ineligible Months: 12 months Contact Information: Contact Name: Ryan Wargo Contact Email: richard.wargo@vcuhealth.org Apply now! Start by sharing your name and email, then proceed to the rest of your application. First Name (required) ad6e155f Last Name (required) 3c558740 Email (required) dbd0c4e1 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting contact information through this form, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive recruitment-related messages such as Job Alerts and recruiter outreach from Virginia Commonwealth University. You may opt-out at any time. Thanks! We're looking forward to receiving your application soon. Send to someone else Submit information for your referral: 24ce015b First Name (required) df737621 Last Name (required) 3e2afe41 Email (required) 35e0e5c2 Share Now! Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting contact information through this form, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive recruitment-related messages such as Job Alerts and recruiter outreach from Virginia Commonwealth University. You may opt-out at any time. Thank you for sharing this job! Recommended for you Digital Accessibility Administrator MCV Main Campus. VCU Main Campus MCV Main Campus. VCU Main Campus Unit: Office Of The Provost MBU Department: Academic Operations Du