    "https://vcujobs.com/jobs/division-chief-of-pediatric-endocrinology-f52940-som-pediatrics-mcv-main-campus-virginia-united-states": {
      "title": "Division Chief of Pediatric Endocrinology - F52940 - SOM/Pediatrics",
      "description": "Division Chief of Pediatric Endocrinology - F52940 - SOM/Pediatrics - MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States Division Chief of Pediatric Endocrinology - F52940 - SOM/Pediatrics - MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States Search Jobs Applicant Login Settings Kiosk mode Ready to join a workforce UN LIKE any other? Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Division Chief of Pediatric Endocrinology - F52940 - SOM/Pediatrics 45001325 907 Floyd Avenue, MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States, 23284 Faculty Clinical Faculty School Of Medicine MBU Advertising Summary: VCU seeks a Pediatric Endocrinology Division Chief to drive innovation, elevate research, and lead a powerhouse clinical team at CHoR. Shape the future of pediatric endocrine care and ignite impact across Virginia. Unit: School of Medicine Department: Pediatrics Mission or Goal of Unit: The Division of Pediatric Endocrinology and Metabolism at the Children's Hospital of Richmond (CHoR) at Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) invites applications and nominations for the position of Division Chief of Pediatric Endocrinology . This is an exceptional opportunity for an accomplished academic pediatric endocrinologist to lead a nationally-recognized division with distinguished clinical, educational, and research programs. The Division of Pediatric Endocrinology is home to the Center for Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism , the region's most comprehensive program for children with diabetes and endocrine disorders, and the Healthy Lifestyles Center , a leading pediatric obesity treatment and research program. The Division offers state-of-the-art, multidisciplinary care encompassing advanced diagnostics, medical nutrition therapy, psychological support, patient and family education, support groups, and participation in clinical trials. The Division's faculty maintain broad clinical and academic interests across pediatric endocrinology and obesity medicine. Its robust research portfolio includes both extramurally-funded investigations and industry-sponsored clinical trials, positioning the Division as a leader in advancing research related to diabetes, obesity, and endocrine disorders. The successful candidate will be a nationally-recognized pediatric endocrinologist with a strong record of clinical excellence, scholarly achievement, mentorship, and leadership. Candidates must be board-certified in Pediatric Endocrinology and eligible for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor within the VCU School of Medicine. Chief Purpose of this Position in Support of Above Mission or Goal: Pediatric Endocrinology plays a vital role in the Department of Pediatrics' educational mission through the training of medical students, residents, fellows, and interprofessional healthcare learners. The Division is home to accredited fellowship programs in both Pediatric Endocrinology and Obesity Medicine and is committed to fostering the next generation of clinician-scientists and academic leaders. Position Overview: The Division Chief is appointed by and reports directly to the Chair of the Department of Pediatrics and collaborates closely with departmental and hospital leadership across CHoR and VCU Health. As administrative leader of the Division, the Chief will provide strategic vision and oversight for all aspects of the Division's clinical, academic, educational, and research missions. The successful candidate will foster a culture of excellence, innovation, collaboration, and accountability while advancing the Division's national reputation. The Division Chief will: Lead strategic planning and growth initiatives for the Division. Maintain an active clinical practice in pediatric endocrinology. Advance quality improvement and patient safety initiatives. Mentor faculty and support professional development and career advancement. Strengthen and expand the Division's educational programs. Foster a collaborative and inclusive environment. Promote scholarly activity and research excellence. Serve as a key partner in departmental and health system leadership efforts. Major Responsibilities: Clinical Leadership Provide direct patient care as a Pediatric Endocrinologist across CHoR clinical settings. Oversee clinical operations for inpatient and outpatient pediatric endocrinology services. Ensure the delivery of evidence-based, high-quality, family-centered care. Participate in after-hours, weekend, and call coverage as appropriate. Lead quality improvement, clinical effectiveness, and operational initiatives within the Division. Academic and Educational Leadership Provide teaching and mentorship to medical students, residents, chief residents, fellows, and advanced practice providers. Support the Division's fellowship training programs in Pediatric Endocrinology and Obesity Medicine. Collaborate with departmental educational leaders to align training programs with LCME, ACGME, and institutional standards. Promote scholarly activity among trainees and faculty. Research Leadership Maintain an active and nationally-recognized research program. Establish and promote a strategic vision for research growth within the Division. Support investigators pursuing basic, translational, clinical, and population health research. Expand opportunities for collaborative and externally funded research initiatives. Enhance the Division's national visibility through scientific presentations, publications, and leadership activities. Service and Professional Contributions Represent the Division within departmental, hospital, and health system committees. Participate in national professional organizations, peer review activities, and scientific meetings. Promote collaborative partnerships throughout CHoR, VCU Health, and the broader healthcare community. Advance outreach efforts and strengthen relationships with pediatric providers throughout Virginia. Administrative Leadership The Division Chief will provide oversight and strategic direction for Division operations and will: Participate in Department of Pediatrics Division Chiefs' meetings and leadership initiatives. Collaborate with program leaders, nursing leadership, and ambulatory services to optimize care delivery. Partner with CHoR and VCU Health leadership to align pediatric endocrine services with organizational priorities. Coordinate educational programs with departmental and institutional leaders. Ensure responsible stewardship of departmental resources and support sustainable growth. Minimum Qualifications: Candidates must possess: Board certification in Pediatrics. Board certification in Pediatric Endocrinology. Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in Virginia. Active DEA registration or eligibility to obtain. Demonstrated clinical excellence in pediatric endocrinology. Leadership experience in an academic, clinical, or programmatic setting. National recognition within pediatric endocrinology. Eligibility for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. Demonstrated ability to work in and foster an environment of respect, professionalism, and civility with a population of faculty, staff, and students from all backgrounds and experiences, or a commitment to do so as a faculty member at VCU. Preferred Qualifications: Preference will be given to candidates with: Experience leading quality improvement initiatives. Formal leadership training or completion of a healthcare leadership development program. Established research expertise in pediatric endocrinology, diabetes, obesity medicine, or related disciplines. A successful history of obtaining extramural research funding. Experience building multidisciplinary clinical and research programs. Salary Range: Commensurate Benefits : All full-time university staff are eligible for VCU’s robust benefits package that includes comprehensive health benefits, paid annual and holiday leave granted up front, generous tuition benefits, retirement planning and savings options, tax-deferred annuity and cash match programs, employee discounts, well-being resources, abundant opportunities for career development and advancement, and more. FLSA Exemption Status: Exempt Restricted Position: No ORP Eligible: Yes Flexible Work Arrangement: Other Rank: Open Tenure: Open Months: 12 months Contact Information: Contact Name: Jewel Jefferson Contact Email: jewel.jefferson@vcuhealth.org Apply now! Start by sharing your name and email, then proceed to the rest of your application. First Name (required) 50067e1c Last Name (required) d9a242a9 Email (required) 5a45dd73 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting contact information through this form, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive recruitment-related messages such as Job Alerts and recruiter outreach from Virginia Commonwealth University. You may opt-out at any time. Thanks! We're looking forward to receiving your application soon. Send to someone else Submit information for your referral: dc75a954 First Name (required) 6fd07dd5 Last Name (required) 2d77bbf3 Email (required) 5ea03eb7 Share Now! Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting contact information through this form, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive recruitment-related messages such as Job Alerts and recruiter outreach from Virginia Commonwealth University. You may opt-out at any time. Thank you for sharing this job! Recommended for you Digital Accessibility Administrator MCV Main Campus. VCU Main Campus MCV Main Campus. VCU Main Campus Unit: Office Of The Provost MBU Department: Academic Operations Duties & Responsibilities: Reporting to Academic Operations within the Office of the Provost and working closely with multiple campus partners, the Digital Accessibility Administra... Senior Research Scientist - Microbiology and Immunology MCV Main Campus MCV Main Campus Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) School of Medicine is a premier academic medical center located in the heart of Richmond. Accounting for almost half of VCU’s sponsored research, the School of Medicine is internationally recognized for patient care and education. Virginia Commonwealth University is an equal opportunity employer. Senior Recruitment Coordinator VCU Main Campus VCU Main Campus The VCU L. Douglas Wilder School of Government and Public Affairs invites applications for a Senior Recruitment Coordinator. Reporting to the Associate Dean for Academic Advising & Student Services, this position serves as the primary school-level point of contact for prospective students, faculty partners, and external partners interested in the L. Douglas Wilder School of Government and Public Affairs. This position drives student enrollment growth, elevates school visibility, and develops strategic recruitment and yield plans aligned with university enrollment strategies and the School’s academic priorities. The role provides strategic oversight for recruitment initiatives, market analysis, and pipeline development across community colleges and higher education institutions in the Mid-Atlantic region. The position directs graduate admissions operations, utilizes recruitment systems (Slate and Tableau), mobilizes faculty and staff engagement, collaborates closely with university leadership and graduate program directors, and leads a team dedicated to attracting a diverse, high-caliber student body. EEO Virginia Commonwealth University is an equal opportunity employer. VCU Notice of Nondiscrimination Know Your Rights: Workplace Discrimination is Illegal VCU's Pay Transparency Nondiscrimination Provision Annual security and fire safety report Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) is committed to assisting all members of the community in providing for their own safety and security. As required by federal law (The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, 20 U.S.C.1092(f), known as the “Clery Act”), the university publishes an Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, which contains information regarding campus security, crime reporting policies, personal safety and crime prevention, and fire and crime statistics for our various campus locations. To request a paper copy of the current report, please contact the VCU Police Department by calling (804) 828-3851 or in person at 224 E. Broad St., Richmond, Virginia 23219. E-verify Notice : After accepting employment, new hires are required to complete an I-9 form and present documentation of their identity and eligibility to work in the United States. Effective June 1, 2011, agencies in the Commonwealth of Virginia's Executive Branch will use the E-Verify system to confirm identity and work authorization. If you have questions or need accommodations during the application and interview process, please contact VCU Human Resources at (804) 828-0177 (Voice), (800) 828-1120 (TDD) or askhr@vcu.edu. The HR Welcome Center on the corner of Belvidere Street and W. Franklin Street is open Monday through Friday 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Before you go... Let's keep in touch! First Name (required) 8b9053f6 Last Name (required) f5895baf Email (required) 60931415 Receive Job Alerts Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting contact information through this form, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive recruitment-related messages such as Job Alerts and recruiter outreach from Virginia Commonwealth University. You may opt-out at any time. Thank you! See you again soon. Close Candidate Details Use of Cookies A cookie is a small amount of data that is sent to your browser from a Web server and stored on the device you used to access the website. The cookie is then sent back to our server each time the browser requests a page from the server. The purpose of cookies is to identify users in order to prepare customized web pages for them and allow VCU to prioritize and improve the content delivered to its site visitors. The following types of cookies may be used on a VCU official site: · Non-Persistent Cookies: Some VCU official sites use non-persistent cookies for the purpose of fulfilling form requests, so as you move through the web pages in that browsing session, the site will remember customized settings, choices made and content selected. These cookies are automatically discarded after closing the web browser. · Persistent Cookies: Some VCU official sites use non-persistent cookies in order to provide improved services and enhance website performance. These cookies help VCU to count the number of visitors to VCU web pages, track visitor engagement on VCU web pages and improve the content VCU provides through its websites. These cookies are not automatically discarded upon browser closure. · Web Beacons and Third-Party Cookies: Some VCU official sites may use web beacons along with third party non-persistent and persistent cookies to collect non-identifiable data from site visitors. VCU uses this collected data to prioritize and improve the content it delivers to its site visitors and to promote the university in a manner that is most relevant to its site visitors. I do not accept I accept"
