    "https://vcujobs.com/jobs/acute-care-surgeon-department-of-surgery-som-m89847-mcv-main-campus-virginia-united-states-south-hill": {
      "title": "Acute Care Surgeon, Department of Surgery, SOM (M89847) - MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States",
      "description": "Acute Care Surgeon, Department of Surgery, SOM (M89847) - MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States - South Hill, Virginia, United States Acute Care Surgeon, Department of Surgery, SOM (M89847) - MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States - South Hill, Virginia, United States Search Jobs Applicant Login Settings Kiosk mode Ready to join a workforce UN LIKE any other? Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Acute Care Surgeon, Department of Surgery, SOM (M89847) 45002716 907 Floyd Avenue, MCV Main Campus, Virginia, United States, 23284 South Hill, Virginia, United States Faculty Clinical Faculty MCVP School Of Medicine MBU Advertising Summary: Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) School of Medicine is a premier academic medical center located in the heart of Richmond. Accounting for almost half of VCU’s sponsored research, the School of Medicine is internationally recognized for patient care and education. Virginia Commonwealth University is an equal opportunity employer. Unit: School Of Medicine MBU Department: Surgery Duties & Responsibilities: Mission The mission of the Division of Acute Care Surgical Services within the Department of Surgery is to provide excellence in clinical care, advance science through research, and educate undergraduate and graduate learners. The Department fosters a culture of academic achievement, professional development, and innovation while providing faculty with robust clinical, educational, and research opportunities. Faculty are expected to contribute to the University, the profession, and the communities we serve. Chief Purpose of this Position The Department of Surgery, Division of Acute Care Surgical Services, is recruiting an Acute Care Surgeon to join our growing faculty. This position offers a unique opportunity to practice across both VCU Medical Center, a Level I Trauma Center and academic referral center in Richmond, Virginia, and Community Memorial Hospital (CMH) in South Hill, Virginia, a growing regional hospital and burgeoning Level III Trauma Center. The faculty member will provide clinical care across Trauma Surgery, Emergency General Surgery, Elective General Surgery, and Surgical Critical Care while supporting the academic, educational, and research missions of the Department. This individual will have the opportunity to practice in both a high-volume academic environment and a growing community-based trauma program, helping expand access to high-quality surgical care across the VCU Health system. Position Responsibilities: Teaching: The faculty member will actively participate in the education and training of medical students, residents, fellows, and other learners. Teaching responsibilities will span all aspects of acute care surgery, including trauma, emergency general surgery, surgical critical care, and elective general surgery. Research: The faculty member will participate in scholarly activity through clinical research, quality improvement initiatives, and collaborative academic projects. Opportunities exist to develop interdisciplinary research partnerships and contribute to publications, presentations, and other scholarly endeavors. Service: Faculty members are expected to contribute to departmental, health system, and university initiatives through committee participation and administrative service. Professional service through involvement in regional and national organizations, peer review activities, and educational programs is encouraged. Clinical: The faculty member will provide clinical care for Trauma Surgery, Emergency General Surgery, Elective General Surgery, and Surgical Critical Care patients at both VCU Medical Center and Community Memorial Hospital. Responsibilities include inpatient and outpatient care, operative management, call coverage, participation in multidisciplinary care teams, and engagement in quality and performance improvement activities. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to contribute to the continued growth of trauma and acute care surgical services at CMH while maintaining an academic surgical practice at VCU Medical Center. Qualifications: Minimum Qualifications MD, DO, or equivalent degree Board Certified in General Surgery Eligibility for medical licensure in Virginia Experience teaching residents and medical students Preferred Qualifications Board Certification or Board Eligibility in Surgical Critical Care Completion of Acute Care Surgery and Surgical Critical Care fellowships Robotic surgery training and experience Interest in clinical research and academic productivity Salary Range: Commensurate FLSA Exemption Status: Exempt ORP Eligible: No Restricted: No Rank: Open Tenure: Ineligible Months: 12 months Contact Information: Contact Name: Mary Waite Contact Email: Mary.Waite@vcuhealth.org Apply now! Start by sharing your name and email, then proceed to the rest of your application. First Name (required) d08d989a Last Name (required) 70995430 Email (required) 17db4d83 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting contact information through this form, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive recruitment-related messages such as Job Alerts and recruiter outreach from Virginia Commonwealth University. You may opt-out at any time. Thanks! We're looking forward to receiving your application soon. Send to someone else Submit information for your referral: 575980da First Name (required) a094b8db Last Name (required) 3738a87e Email (required) 40d83c68 Share Now! Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting contact information through this form, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive recruitment-related messages such as Job Alerts and recruiter outreach from Virginia Commonwealth University. You may opt-out at any time. Thank you for sharing this job! Recommended for you (Rolling Pool) English Language Program Adjunct Instructor - Global Education Office VCU Main Campus VCU Main Campus The English Language Program (ELP) in the Global Education Office at Virginia Commonwealth University seeks qualified part-time adjunct faculty to teach in its intensive English language program. The ELP provides English language support to students who are academically admissible to VCU undergraduate and graduate programs but who need additional English language proficiency to succeed in an academic environment. The program also serves individuals seeking to improve their English language skills for educational, professional, and personal purposes, including prospective students who may later apply to VCU academic programs. Reporting to the Director of the English Language Program, adjunct faculty play an important role in supporting student language development and academic success while contributing to a welcoming and inclusive learning environment. Plastic, Reconstructive & Burn Surgery Research, Department of Surgery, SOM (F80610) MCV Main Campus MCV Main Campus Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) School of Medicine is a premier academic medical center located in the heart of Richmond. Accounting for almost half of VCU’s sponsored research, the School of Medicine is internationally recognized for patient care and education. Virginia Commonwealth University is an equal opportunity employer. Surgical Oncology / Breast Cancer Research, Department of Surgery, SOM (F80620) MCV Main Campus MCV Main Campus Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) School of Medicine is a premier academic medical center located in the heart of Richmond. Accounting for almost half of VCU’s sponsored research, the School of Medicine is internationally recognized for patient care and education. Virginia Commonwealth University is an equal opportunity employer. EEO Virginia Commonwealth University is an equal opportunity employer. VCU Notice of Nondiscrimination Know Your Rights: Workplace Discrimination is Illegal VCU's Pay Transparency Nondiscrimination Provision Annual security and fire safety report Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) is committed to assisting all members of the community in providing for their own safety and security. As required by federal law (The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, 20 U.S.C.1092(f), known as the “Clery Act”), the university publishes an Annual Security and Fire Safety Report, which contains information regarding campus security, crime reporting policies, personal safety and crime prevention, and fire and crime statistics for our various campus locations. To request a paper copy of the current report, please contact the VCU Police Department by calling (804) 828-3851 or in person at 224 E. Broad St., Richmond, Virginia 23219. E-verify Notice : After accepting employment, new hires are required to complete an I-9 form and present documentation of their identity and eligibility to work in the United States. Effective June 1, 2011, agencies in the Commonwealth of Virginia's Executive Branch will use the E-Verify system to confirm identity and work authorization. If you have questions or need accommodations during the application and interview process, please contact VCU Human Resources at (804) 828-0177 (Voice), (800) 828-1120 (TDD) or askhr@vcu.edu. The HR Welcome Center on the corner of Belvidere Street and W. Franklin Street is open Monday through Friday 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Before you go... Let's keep in touch! First Name (required) cb3a89db Last Name (required) 75ecb75e Email (required) b634dd5d Receive Job Alerts Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting contact information through this form, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive recruitment-related messages such as Job Alerts and recruiter outreach from Virginia Commonwealth University. You may opt-out at any time. Thank you! See you again soon. Close Candidate Details Use of Cookies A cookie is a small amount of data that is sent to your browser from a Web server and stored on the device you used to access the website. The cookie is then sent back to our server each time the browser requests a page from the server. The purpose of cookies is to identify users in order to prepare customized web pages for them and allow VCU to prioritize and improve the content delivered to its site visitors. The following types of cookies may be used on a VCU official site: · Non-Persistent Cookies: Some VCU official sites use non-persistent cookies for the purpose of fulfilling form requests, so as you move through the web pages in that browsing session, the site will remember customized settings, choices made and content selected. These cookies are automatically discarded after closing the web browser. · Persistent Cookies: Some VCU official sites use non-persistent cookies in order to provide improved services and enhance website performance. These cookies help VCU to count the number of visitors to VCU web pages, track visitor engagement on VCU web pages and improve the content VCU provides through its websites. These cookies are not automatically discarded upon browser closure. · Web Beacons and Third-Party Cookies: Some VCU official sites may use web beacons along with third party non-persistent and persistent cookies to collect non-identifiable data from site visitors. VCU uses this collected data to prioritize and improve the content it delivers to its site visitors and to promote the university in a manner that is most relevant to its site visitors. I do not accept I accept"
